ion/worked-solution "At $t=0,$ the supplied model gives $Q(0)=\\alpha(1-0/50)+\\beta(1-0/50)^{3}=\\alpha+\\beta.$ The initial amount is $34\\,\\text{kg},$ so $\\alpha+\\beta=34.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e1e846-9e05-501b-974e-8642dacc9f03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "228.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672808} {:answer/id #uuid "96899a85-e81a-5fa2-9faf-596f72428877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "224.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672824} {:answer/id #uuid "9c03c3f7-70d4-5faa-9e3b-ecaa003c3baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "226.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672826} {:answer/id #uuid "ca007285-9488-5d69-931c-3c1cdba5a712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "223.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672833} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0dd9a1-8939-534b-8fe1-61130a258c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "225.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672837} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8238105-55b4-50ef-b094-4a132b322bef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4d24b72e-d25f-59d2-bb3e-c6bd67f97b9c" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $190\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $50\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =18\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $50\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=50+15t-10t=5(10+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q=18.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(10+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|10+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(10+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q) & =(10+t)^{2}\\cdot 18 \\\\ (10+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(10+t)Q & =18(10+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2}) & =18(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 18(10+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(10+t)^{2} & =18\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(10+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =6(10+t)+\\frac{C}{(10+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ 6(10+0)+\\frac{C}{(10+0)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ 60+\\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =-40 \\\\ C & =-40(10)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =6(10+t)-\\frac{40(10)^{2}}{(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =6(10+t)-40{\\left(\\frac{10}{10+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $190\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=190$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =190 \\\\ 50+15{t}_{o}-10{t}_{o} & =190 \\\\ 50+5{t}_{o} & =190 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =140 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =28\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(28) & =6(10+28)-40{\\left(\\frac{10}{10+28}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =228-40{\\left(\\frac{10}{38}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈225.2\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37d3dbfe-81e6-5c4b-bc37-f60fb4beb15e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "238.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672810} {:answer/id #uuid "3845663d-6c8b-564f-991e-5525391b388d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "241.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672811} {:answer/id #uuid "9505e145-3829-51e6-af55-4ca05fb593e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "242.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672823} {:answer/id #uuid "983c0311-04b0-5af4-b7ae-1d85c98e4775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "240.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672825} {:answer/id #uuid "9c112657-3a58-5227-8060-681920d3d89d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "239.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672827} :answer-field/id #uuid "53445c49-a61b-5717-8a8f-2ba5cfb737e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "566ddc14-35df-5f2a-8371-ba5cac1be4c4" :question/problem "An ink reservoir for an industrial printer can hold up to $249\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of ink solution with $80\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved ink dye. A more concentrated ink solution with concentration $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir minus the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of ink dye enters the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *ink dye* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =9\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+9t-6t=3(40+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(40+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *ink dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(40+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(40+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(40+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{40+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{2Q}{40+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{40+t}\\,Q=9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(40+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|40+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(40+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(40+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(40+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(40+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{40+t}\\,Q) & =(40+t)^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\ (40+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(40+t)Q & =9(40+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(40+t)^{2}) & =9(40+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(40+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 9(40+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(40+t)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(40+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(40+t)+\\frac{C}{(40+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $80\\,\\text{lb}$ of ink dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=80,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =80 \\\\ 3(40+0)+\\frac{C}{(40+0)^{2}} & =80 \\\\ 120+\\frac{C}{(40)^{2}} & =80 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{40}^{2}} & =-40 \\\\ C & =-40(40)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(40+t)-\\frac{40(40)^{2}}{(40+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3(40+t)-40{\\left(\\frac{40}{40+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $249\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=249$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =249 \\\\ 120+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =249 \\\\ 120+3{t}_{o} & =249 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =129 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =43\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(43) & =3(40+43)-40{\\left(\\frac{40}{40+43}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =249-40{\\left(\\frac{40}{83}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈239.7\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4a3eff-430a-5254-b33c-e4b7a1ce9007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "383.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672806} {:answer/id #uuid "25f1ca40-bbb2-585e-b802-e7abc4b0609a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "381.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672809} {:answer/id #uuid "3f66ff27-185c-5ed0-968e-616134064bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "379.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672812} {:answer/id #uuid "700b78b2-e9d9-511c-b04d-6cf0070d3cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "378.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672819} {:answer/id #uuid "dafbce03-3f81-585d-94d3-c444a4c0b2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "382.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672834}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672809} :answer-field/id #uuid "0939b42c-050b-5b2d-a3aa-812a8cbc4426" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b9bb014c-8036-50d5-98f7-cb585fa07997" :question/problem "A dye mixing tank at a textile factory can hold up to $420\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution with $120\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved dye. A more concentrated dye solution with concentration $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =36\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $30\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=300+30t-20t=10(30+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{10(30+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{10(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{10(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{10(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =36-\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q=36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(30+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q) & =(30+t)^{2}\\cdot 36 \\\\ (30+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(30+t)Q & =36(30+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2}) & =36(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 36(30+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30+t)^{2} & =36\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(30+t)+\\frac{C}{(30+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $120\\,\\text{lb}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=120,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =120 \\\\ 12(30+0)+\\frac{C}{(30+0)^{2}} & =120 \\\\ 360+\\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =120 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =-240 \\\\ C & =-240(30)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(30+t)-\\frac{240(30)^{2}}{(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =12(30+t)-240{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $420\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=420$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =420 \\\\ 300+30{t}_{o}-20{t}_{o} & =420 \\\\ 300+10{t}_{o} & =420 \\\\ 10{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =12\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(12) & =12(30+12)-240{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+12}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =504-240{\\left(\\frac{30}{42}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈381.6\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50c05219-e146-5c0b-9f47-8478e9cc17eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "73.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672815} {:answer/id #uuid "58e5327d-e065-5983-b7c0-80260b2d7837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "77.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672818} {:answer/id #uuid "79055611-d13a-5b4e-b215-47c495ddcd57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "75.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672820} {:answer/id #uuid "815ad045-92f6-5634-9a68-0ba13cc2bea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672822} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cc719f-91cb-5ba1-9d7a-0694870d0ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "71.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672822} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f0a520-4075-560e-ac9e-5f9c2b76fd9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c4c7ec47-5290-53ac-8dc7-8af5327e3c22" :question/problem "A coolant reservoir in a manufacturing plant can hold up to $184\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of coolant solution with $15\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved corrosion inhibitor. A more concentrated coolant solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir minus the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of inhibitor enters the reservoir at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *inhibitor* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+12t-8t=4(30+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *inhibitor* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q=6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(30+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q) & =(30+t)^{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\ (30+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(30+t)Q & =6(30+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2}) & =6(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 6(30+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30+t)^{2} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2(30+t)+\\frac{C}{(30+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $15\\,\\text{lb}$ of inhibitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=15,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =15 \\\\ 2(30+0)+\\frac{C}{(30+0)^{2}} & =15 \\\\ 60+\\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =15 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{30}^{2}} & =-45 \\\\ C & =-45(30)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2(30+t)-\\frac{45(30)^{2}}{(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2(30+t)-45{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $184\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=184$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =184 \\\\ 120+12{t}_{o}-8{t}_{o} & =184 \\\\ 120+4{t}_{o} & =184 \\\\ 4{t}_{o} & =64 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =16\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(16) & =2(30+16)-45{\\left(\\frac{30}{30+16}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =92-45{\\left(\\frac{30}{46}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈72.9\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144981e1-0af5-524f-b755-723ec92ceafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "497.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672805} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9ed5b4-4935-583e-a40d-801dceb367ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "493.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672807} {:answer/id #uuid "80951659-772c-51af-88a0-1b9a88f45896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "495.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672821} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f73285-229e-5601-bd7e-d317529e0561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "495.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672829} {:answer/id #uuid "ed696f17-3d6d-50f3-a6d4-1a3ad80e8f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "496.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672821} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6e5f789-32c8-5623-8da5-2970434753ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ff58189c-6e25-5dd9-8cd1-371a00326207" :question/problem "A fertilizer tank for an irrigation system can hold up to $352\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $176\\,\\text{gal}$ of fertilizer solution with $132\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved fertilizer. A more concentrated solution with concentration $1.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $16\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of fertilizer enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *fertilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =36\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $176\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $24\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $16\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=176+24t-16t=8(22+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{8(22+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *fertilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(16\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16Q}{8(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{22+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =36-\\frac{2Q}{22+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{22+t}\\,Q=36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(22+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|22+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(22+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(22+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(22+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(22+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{22+t}\\,Q) & =(22+t)^{2}\\cdot 36 \\\\ (22+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(22+t)Q & =36(22+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(22+t)^{2}) & =36(22+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(22+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 36(22+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(22+t)^{2} & =36\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(22+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(22+t)+\\frac{C}{(22+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $132\\,\\text{lb}$ of fertilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=132,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =132 \\\\ 12(22+0)+\\frac{C}{(22+0)^{2}} & =132 \\\\ 264+\\frac{C}{(22)^{2}} & =132 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{22}^{2}} & =-132 \\\\ C & =-132(22)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(22+t)-\\frac{132(22)^{2}}{(22+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =12(22+t)-132{\\left(\\frac{22}{22+t}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $352\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=352$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =352 \\\\ 176+24{t}_{o}-16{t}_{o} & =352 \\\\ 176+8{t}_{o} & =352 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =176 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =22\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(22) & =12(22+22)-132{\\left(\\frac{22}{22+22}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =528-132{\\left(\\frac{22}{44}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈495.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247aea16-5a0e-53c2-9eab-769593e0321b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=10.8-4.8e^{-t/30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672842} {:answer/id #uuid "4a75fa10-6da7-5407-9c8d-f142fc65c9f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=18-8e^{-t/24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672848} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf6c63e-69eb-5222-be8b-10c3f1be8e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=18-8e^{-t/30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672850} {:answer/id #uuid "9294db4d-c049-5c7e-9775-3e4ab32bdfb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=10.8-0.8e^{-t/24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672860} {:answer/id #uuid "d583b770-9481-5579-8d91-684112cd4467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=18-12e^{-t/24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672842} :answer-field/id #uuid "f72c24e8-2705-5501-b569-59c87bcb3ce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1b80a0f4-7801-535e-bc20-eae6ddee5773" :question/problem "A brewing tank initially holds $360\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of sugar $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.36\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $360\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{360}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{360}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{360}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{30}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.36-\\frac{Q}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{30}Q=0.36.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{30}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/30},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{30}\\alpha =0.36\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =10.8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=10.8.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/30}+10.8.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/30}+10.8 & =6 \\\\ A & =-4.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=10.8-4.8{e}^{-t/30}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2672e6b2-eafa-5b83-9c60-d31c4e3baaed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=14-2e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672843} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6606fe-295f-54d9-a96b-ece95eaa67cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=21-3e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672852} {:answer/id #uuid "a57aeafe-7041-5c00-8fd6-c39c93acb22f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=14+4e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672863} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e7feec-a4a9-5925-8994-9b16f6261677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=21-3e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672864} {:answer/id #uuid "f524c191-98bd-54bf-8c61-18cfacadfeed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=21-9e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672864} :answer-field/id #uuid "669474bd-f365-5bcf-8161-9f793e84c972" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "387eb92e-520a-5684-94a0-8f6b898f8d01" :question/problem "A storage tank initially holds $350\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant.\nA feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the disinfectant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of disinfectant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of disinfectant in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the tank minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the disinfectant enters the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $350\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{350}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=1.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =1.5\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =21.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=21.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+21.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+21 & =18 \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=21-3{e}^{-t/14}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a120c58-a242-55df-ae6b-10b365f315f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=16-e^{-t/80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672847} {:answer/id #uuid "63be0667-c58b-5165-b1b6-6499626e2f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=16+9e^{-t/16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672853} {:answer/id #uuid "74120287-fbd3-5aed-838e-a58eb85a7813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=24+e^{-t/80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672856} {:answer/id #uuid "9d11c0ba-204a-532b-a292-0fbe25eff11e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=24-9e^{-t/16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672862} {:answer/id #uuid "e0be752c-5cae-56aa-a99b-5e2e3da84b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=24-9e^{-t/80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672871} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cab0c71-5a81-5ddc-9dfe-02c242f74b7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "79b642d6-727a-5f0d-9531-4fcd626dce40" :question/problem "A coolant tank initially holds $800\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive.\nA feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive is pumped into the tank at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of additive $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of additive in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additive enters the tank minus the rate at which additive leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additive enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive enters the tank at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *additive* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additive exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $800\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of additive in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ *additive* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{800}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{80}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.3-\\frac{Q}{80}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80}Q=0.3.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/80},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{80}\\alpha =0.3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=24.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/80}+24.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=15,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =15 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/80}+24 & =15 \\\\ A & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=24-9{e}^{-t/80}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f327130-ce5a-5579-9f17-3c81140d18c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=60-46e^{-t/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672851} {:answer/id #uuid "9071fcc3-5fc2-5104-a059-5fc677809145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=10+4e^{-t/40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672858} {:answer/id #uuid "91d23fe2-88cf-561c-a0e4-1741996358a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=10+5e^{-t/25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672859} {:answer/id #uuid "ae63b870-5af2-55d8-954f-51150459ff29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=10+5e^{-t/40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672866} {:answer/id #uuid "f23c15db-b163-510c-9170-9872773ac7af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=60-45e^{-t/40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672872}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672866} :answer-field/id #uuid "31456c23-1619-532c-936a-7b4aab6fde98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bd866fe0-d825-5d93-8387-02823cb34e5f" :question/problem "A brewing tank initially holds $1000\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA feed solution containing $0.01\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of sugar $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.01\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the sugar enters the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.25\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $1000\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{1000}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{1000}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{1000}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{1000}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{40}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.25-\\frac{Q}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}Q=0.25.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{40}\\alpha =0.25\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=10.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40}+10.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=15,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =15 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/40}+10 & =15 \\\\ A & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=10+5{e}^{-t/40}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44b353c1-c2ee-54c3-b666-ccb5c859c5f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=8.4-4.4e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672846} {:answer/id #uuid "67f70dc6-1daf-5923-90ad-76fd65eed828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=25.2-20.2e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672855} {:answer/id #uuid "996a9731-acc5-5b69-8d22-f2fd2ac623d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=25.2-20.2e^{-t/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672861} {:answer/id #uuid "ae638f6f-9aab-5257-8412-79161e72772d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=8.4-3.4e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672865} {:answer/id #uuid "d9dc6910-635d-50c4-b176-6fbb2ac614b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q(t)=25.2-21.2e^{-t/35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672870}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672846} :answer-field/id #uuid "23514375-6fae-594a-b5a7-0e55f8d76eee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d3e93811-bde2-50b4-a162-641048dbfc47" :question/problem "A coolant tank initially holds $420\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive.\nA feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive is pumped into the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of additive $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of additive in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additive enters the tank minus the rate at which additive leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additive enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.02\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *additive* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.02\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additive exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $420\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of additive in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *additive* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =8.4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=8.4.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+8.4.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=4,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =4 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+8.4 & =4 \\\\ A & =-4.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=8.4-4.4{e}^{-t/14}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3851a9f2-0b5d-53dd-93d7-ca309444885e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{27}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674023} {:answer/id #uuid "6eed8781-b826-5b49-ad6d-8dddc59fbde8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{17}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{11}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674030} {:answer/id #uuid "7e43f1cc-a0f5-53b0-9743-3a6e0a11befe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{27}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674032} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e4363c-4a28-568b-a4bd-3a866763e340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674043} {:answer/id #uuid "e91c3a86-7c5b-570d-a0d0-abbaafe6b6de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{23}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674046}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674032} :answer-field/id #uuid "a19a8de7-aa28-5572-9f4f-30ab4d594c04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4f4e7729-4448-5202-a479-0d1795c351cb" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=- \\frac{27}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(-3)=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2}-(3)=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=-3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(\\frac{3}{2})-(-3)=- \\frac{27}{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=- \\frac{27}{2}-(-3)-(3)=- \\frac{27}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=- \\frac{27}{2}$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=-3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{27}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c691cbf-d3a7-56be-9269-9915617e50e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{500}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674194} {:answer/id #uuid "72f1279a-9b66-52a1-8b7f-0adc29014b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674205} {:answer/id #uuid "a6911744-3cc0-580b-bfcf-73c927ca18e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674209} {:answer/id #uuid "cd034e13-d7d0-570b-8247-6c28837fa17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674217} {:answer/id #uuid "fe37c4cf-81b2-5e7d-a4ac-216d9373b83c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674223}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674205} :answer-field/id #uuid "55213294-9c90-5c12-b6da-b4a46e8e1b70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0bbd5512-c745-5506-9589-e476017d1f98" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=16.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 6 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 6 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 6 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=16.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 4,16).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-4\\le u\\le 4,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 16\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/38/387e22e062c4ff7f24d26561fe369041f11f59a2b0305f8dc7b3ce49d411031a.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-6\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{16}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}[v]_{u^{2}}^{16}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-4}^{4}(16 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 16 u]_{-4}^{4} \\\\ &=1(((64)+\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right))-((-64)+\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{256}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1931099c-2d1b-5af8-80f7-090cbab249db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674192} {:answer/id #uuid "4731326e-9b9a-5ff6-aff3-587c51ebaef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674202} {:answer/id #uuid "a8be08e9-1501-58b0-8bd0-b24641111816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674210} {:answer/id #uuid "e0af6abf-9221-5426-a3e2-2fa1d2bc4842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674219} {:answer/id #uuid "f915a4c0-c1a0-5652-8118-969be9da31e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674202} :answer-field/id #uuid "b54915ac-6f13-58bd-a737-8c93b908a255" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2180d312-6f4f-5d5b-86a4-02dad7f687e5" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=4.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,4).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/b4/b43c84eb76241233dc88e2d6a94bc4e5e24d558f8c9f46a826d0d928059aefc2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-1\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{4}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[v]_{u^{2}}^{4}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 4 u]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=1(((8)+\\left(- \\frac{8}{3}\\right))-((-8)+\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{32}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32399fa1-20c9-5361-9d96-1f602b22d25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674195} {:answer/id #uuid "3744cd2b-88d1-57a5-a1d8-0dcb12bb78fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{500}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674198} {:answer/id #uuid "37aec481-2bc1-550b-93f1-2b0994165854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674199} {:answer/id #uuid "8b45065e-84be-59b1-9c57-ddb9e4cda6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674208} {:answer/id #uuid "cae629d2-0e63-5ef8-b6e5-875bb407408a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674216}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674198} :answer-field/id #uuid "3588ef6d-be33-5b91-9ae2-77a70c5b4721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "596a3028-8196-5628-a399-013186bd1729" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=25.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=25.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=25\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 5,25).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-5\\le u\\le 5,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 25\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ca/ca54518b097929ecf8756bc699c5fef32de07b174134781e778fdcc395e964fe.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{25}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}[v]_{u^{2}}^{25}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}(25)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-5}^{5}(25 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 25 u]_{-5}^{5} \\\\ &=1(((125)+\\left(- \\frac{125}{3}\\right))-((-125)+\\left(\\frac{125}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{500}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{500}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11db1db5-5a0f-5fb0-a754-1b0a0fcb5fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674191} {:answer/id #uuid "3931285c-aa25-5248-91c8-d02a7c61aa64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674200} {:answer/id #uuid "7cbfebff-3fb6-5acc-83eb-383404c2df9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674207} {:answer/id #uuid "af393de9-3626-52b1-b53c-f870b9f3bd48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674212} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f2f462-d03e-54dd-8a98-bb5737baebc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{500}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674215}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674191} :answer-field/id #uuid "78c37a21-b63d-5674-9fe8-7c4cc3fd9598" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bfbb3e6e-4a50-5fa2-b9fd-b83c3046c21c" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=36.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=36.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=36\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 6,36).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-6\\le u\\le 6,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 36\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/13/132cec4e3586ef176e2c94e05bbe8860063cce2cef4d0835159da844ea43b8ae.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{36}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}[v]_{u^{2}}^{36}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}(36)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-6}^{6}(36 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 36 u]_{-6}^{6} \\\\ &=1(((216)+(-72))-((-216)+(72))) \\\\ &=1(288) \\\\ &=288\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f2e466-85ea-5119-8556-d5125458bf34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674197} {:answer/id #uuid "419f5596-9ef8-5fd6-9f40-b432bbde2132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674201} {:answer/id #uuid "aa93be78-621b-5dba-a958-5824fd7c1535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674211} {:answer/id #uuid "d474f446-afd7-5635-942c-718b8fb2cb61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674218} {:answer/id #uuid "e723eff8-04b7-501d-8051-1b9d4ca4a3fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{500}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674211} :answer-field/id #uuid "04a58d4d-d91f-5e80-8080-2159132921d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec1bbf79-9916-53c6-8b93-5585f4221005" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0$ and the line $y=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x - 8 y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\nSubstituting $x - 8 y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n(x - 8 y)^{2}-(y)=0\\,⟹\\,u^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v=u^{2}.\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\nv=4.\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &=u^{2} \\\\ v &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,u=\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,4).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,u^{2}\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/b4/b43c84eb76241233dc88e2d6a94bc4e5e24d558f8c9f46a826d0d928059aefc2.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x}&\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\\\\\frac{∂v}{∂x}&\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&-8\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D dxdy &=\\iint_Δ |\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,dudv \\\\ &=\\iint_Δ |1|\\,dudv \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[\\int_{u^{2}}^{4}dv]du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}[v]_{u^{2}}^{4}du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4)-(u^{2})du \\\\ &=1\\int_{-2}^{2}(4 - u^{2})du \\\\ &=1[- \\frac{u^{3}}{3} + 4 u]_{-2}^{2} \\\\ &=1(((8)+\\left(- \\frac{8}{3}\\right))-((-8)+\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right))) \\\\ &=1\\left(\\frac{32}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "255f9156-287e-5cc9-b8ed-eb31b15e83ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674307}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674307} :answer-field/id #uuid "b024a65a-2e57-571c-a34d-025276659956" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a831727-2211-5532-89c4-e5a4ccb9ae4a" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-7x+16}$ at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-7x+16.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot -7 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{-7x+16}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{-7x+16}})|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7}{2\\sqrt{-7(0)+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7}{2\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "767fa440-8fc1-5358-9025-7c8125e6aaa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674312} :answer-field/id #uuid "099a5827-9c3f-5cf3-bff5-003f736749ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2cf444c8-3239-54ca-9516-8df227c41a5f" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}$ at the point where $x=3$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2{x}^{2}+6x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4x+6.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (4x+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2x+3)}{2\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+3}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(\\frac{2x+3}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x}})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(3)+6}{2\\sqrt{2(3{)}^{2}+6(3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{2\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c11f32b-69c1-5fd0-bddb-c652f4dd99dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674313} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3399efe-ec83-56d2-bfb3-9ba448fc95b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "309f479d-646b-5563-8225-cb5faadfd4a1" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-7x}$ at the point where $x=4$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4{x}^{2}-7x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x-7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (8x-7) \\\\  & =\\frac{8x-7}{2\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-7x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8x-7}{2\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-7x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & ={(\\frac{8x-7}{2\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-7x}})|}_{x=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(4)-7}{2\\sqrt{4(4{)}^{2}-7(4)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e15b33e8-c7f6-5a5e-93f2-cb68037c61a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674321} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0342523-f1be-5f79-85fa-6dd2455256a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "86491180-1d5e-5901-bd2b-dafda235930e" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}$ at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-5{x}^{2}+9x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9-10x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (9-10x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10x-9}{2\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10x-9}{2\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(-\\frac{10x-9}{2\\sqrt{-5{x}^{2}+9x}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-10(1)}{2\\sqrt{-5(1{)}^{2}+9(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3af54b83-704e-55d5-a89d-14d9c5f3ff86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674310} {:answer/id #uuid "482d0285-39be-5f4b-8137-eea61ed52b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{39}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674311} {:answer/id #uuid "80cc6b4d-e037-5552-9468-63b241864f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674314} {:answer/id #uuid "e031eaae-76f9-58af-b3e6-50abf25c826b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674320} {:answer/id #uuid "e938f3d1-5a47-5d48-988a-c15b0f05149d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674323}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674320} :answer-field/id #uuid "c28412ef-227e-5ded-9964-e41d96174b86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e8ccbb82-8efb-534c-acab-7f6468bd8bed" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{37+7x}$ at the point where $x=-4.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=37+7x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (7) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{37+7x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-4} & ={(\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{37+7x}})|}_{x=-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{37+7(-4)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08a80692-07d1-5e95-9163-360168a6b971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674510} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9e38e1-5a4e-5c22-90fe-cf7b1a5825fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674512} {:answer/id #uuid "0e54f482-2092-5feb-85db-4583e158d871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674513} {:answer/id #uuid "111dd5e5-6781-51f0-801d-9a1be7bce501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674514} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa6cc93-c4ad-5135-80dc-1cf5f92f65dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674525}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674512} :answer-field/id #uuid "0900970a-ee9c-567b-b076-c4bbdc85b4ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3f463f23-c4c0-50ef-b787-a5f651885d0d" :question/problem "Given that $y=-\\frac{\\ln x}{5}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{\\ln x}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\ln x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bfa219c-8885-5d36-8bfc-8d0a5511dfcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674518} {:answer/id #uuid "c98d7561-b6f7-5754-83d8-e9cfb5fa49fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674532} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd9c2c3-53ec-5824-a961-7501d750f635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674533} {:answer/id #uuid "df4ae5b8-2157-5ea1-b90d-c812f450d86a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674534} {:answer/id #uuid "ef7c4f79-c46c-5ba9-88f8-226f84de67af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674538}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674534} :answer-field/id #uuid "18cd1fe6-7cb8-5eff-8148-ebb2d9a0d433" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4c022b5d-98f2-5234-a6f7-a40dac12da23" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{\\ln x}{8}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\ln x}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\ln x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16790f4f-c578-5c4c-bb13-6d8167e3706a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674515}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674515} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4f40a1-50bd-5844-8a18-b9a27d7bf9b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4de1eb25-975c-5983-98bc-143b768ecda1" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{11}{6}\\ln x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{11}{6}\\ln x) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln x) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6396aa68-2475-5bc0-ae66-1b54b8ffd551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674523} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6cbf6f-ca51-552e-85f8-8c197b700f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674527} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d39ccb-611c-5b07-8dcc-ea9888eefe2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674529} {:answer/id #uuid "e8fd4a97-0900-5176-a631-023a704f9690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674535} {:answer/id #uuid "e9767aff-e5ea-59f4-bd98-1633369812c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674536}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674535} :answer-field/id #uuid "febe7a69-bf00-5638-9f11-25b82e2ae826" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6f161feb-d535-5282-961a-0fa5779f2747" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{\\ln x}{9}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\ln x}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\ln x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0416c6db-d849-5b0b-897c-017d1824e3b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674509} {:answer/id #uuid "1c19d29c-2035-57cf-b6d2-d06f71a38508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{21x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674517} {:answer/id #uuid "324059dc-f7c3-5824-96e8-c019ead5de9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674519} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae9c00b-df77-5303-a41a-c1e287453f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674526} {:answer/id #uuid "eae92518-1053-5bf5-997b-200b5dd97071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186674519} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ea01571-ea79-5220-9446-55090a760228" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186674528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c0a22e18-faf7-5870-acc2-2cc085f612e9" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\frac{21}{2}\\ln x$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{21}{2}\\ln x) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\ln x) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186676397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57ccf1c6-9ca0-5204-9f08-d93f36ae7cb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf751180